   "id": "h2-45",
   "name": "Kwashiorkor and marasmus: children at risk and prevention"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Children most at risk: those from 6 months to 5 years, especially at weaning; children in poor families or areas of war, drought and famine; children with frequent infections, measles or diarrhoea; children whose mother is ill or absent; twins and children born close together.\nPrevention:\n• breastfeed babies only for the first six months, then continue breastfeeding while adding other foods;\n• give weaning foods rich in protein: pap enriched with soya, groundnut or egg; beans, fish, crayfish, eggs, milk;\n• give balanced, frequent meals;\n• vaccinate children and treat diarrhoea early (oral rehydration salts);\n• weigh children regularly at the health centre (growth chart);\n• teach mothers about nutrition; grow vegetables, beans and soya; keep chickens for eggs;\n• space births.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
